[33:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'our plasticine scene. Truly impressive things. Now, I want to briefly mention how you can improve your results even further. And this involves making adjustments during'

[33:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editing. What I do is place my original generation on an empty timeline in Premiere. Then I add cut points at each moment where the scene changes to a'

[33:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different camera angle. Next, I overlap the clips, which allows me to create J or L cuts where each shot links to the one before and after it. And we can also fine-tune'

[33:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things by eliminating some of that awkward dead space between each shot. You\'ll notice that I\'m also adding fades to each of the cuts in the audio track. And again, this will simply make each take blend'

[33:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into the previous and next one much better than what you get directly from C Dance 2.5. To put this in context, let me first show you our original generation. Jack!'

[34:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'What\'s wrong? You need to find a better place to store this stupid sword. It is not safe for children. And why on earth is it so big? Do'

[34:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'n\'t know. It is completely impractical. Please get rid of it. The house is already messy enough'

[34:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as it is. And now I\'ll show you my re-edition. Jack! What\'s wrong? You need to find a better place to store this stupid sword. It\'s not'

[34:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'safe for children, and why on earth is it so big? Do n\'t know. It is completely impractical.'

[35:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Please get rid of it. The house is already messy enough as it is. It feels much better, doesn\'t it? The original duration of the generation was 30 seconds and, after my editing, it was reduced to 23 seconds. By losing those extra 7 seconds, the whole scene'

[35:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feels much more agile and natural. I encourage you to treat your AI generations like shots on a film set. Try to adopt the mindset of a director. Just because they have a'

[35:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'full take, doesn\'t mean they should use the whole thing. Carefully select the moments that work really well. Another great finishing touch we can add to the editing is to use an effect called "posterize time". This'

[35:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might have a different name in your editing program, but basically what we\'ll be doing is reducing the frame rate of the sequence. An original generation straight from'

[35:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'SeeDance 2.5 reaches 24 frames per second. Using "posterize time", I reduce that by half, until I get to 12 FPS. And this is really going to exaggerate that stop-motion animation style.'

[36:07] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Claymation characters in a restaurant with a monster outside the window.
AUDIO: 'The viewer will feel each individual frame a little more, and that will make everything feel less perfect and more human in the process. This, of course, is a purely'

[36:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stylistic choice. You might like that kind of fluid movement that you get straight from SeeDance 2.5. Another trick many AI filmmakers use is to add a layer of film grain over their generations.'

[36:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I think this is generally more important when dealing with something photorealistic. Obviously, with our style of plastisol molding it\'s a bit more flexible, but it adds that extra sense of texture. What I\'m trying to say is that you shouldn\'t just take'

[36:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your original generations and use them as is . You can elevate your projects significantly by dedicating a little more work to editing. And as you can see, it\'s not always about doing something technically complex. Small'

[37:01] [scene:talk]
